@node Invocation
@chapter Invocation

@cindex invocation
@cindex command line
The general form is:

@example
cgra [-d DEVICE] [-c FILE] COMMAND [ARGS...] [OUTPUT OPTIONS]
@end example

Global options (@option{-d}, @option{-c}) and output options may appear
anywhere on the line; the first non-option word is the command.

@section Device resolution
@cindex device resolution
@cindex -d
The device is chosen, in order of precedence:

@enumerate
@item the @option{-d}/@option{--device} argument;
@item the @env{CGRA_DEVICE} environment variable;
@item the @code{default} device profile.
@end enumerate

@noindent
The resolved name may be a @dfn{profile} defined in a @file{.cgra} file, or a
literal: a serial path (anything containing @samp{/}, e.g.@:
@file{/dev/ttyUSB1}), the emulator @samp{sim:} (or @samp{sim:flaky} to force
the retry path), or @samp{auto}---the port saved by @command{cgra probe}.  A
profile's port and baud can be overridden by @env{CGRA_PORT} and
@env{CGRA_BAUD} (@pxref{Environment}).

@section Input vectors
@cindex input vectors
@cindex SRC
Commands that consume vectors accept a @dfn{source} (@var{SRC}) in three forms:

@table @asis
@item inline
a quoted list, @code{"1 2 3 4"} (whitespace- or comma-separated; @code{0x}/@code{0}
prefixes give hex/octal).
@item @code{@@file}
read the numbers from @file{file}.
@item @code{-}
read from standard input.
@end table

@noindent
Sources are given with @option{--a} and @option{--b} (and @option{-m}/@option{--x}
for matrix commands).  If @option{--a} is omitted, positional numbers are used;
if there are none, the vector is read from standard input---so
@code{seq 0 255 | cgra run relu -d sim} works.

@section Output formatting
@cindex output formatting
@cindex --io
@cindex --json
Results are printed one line per vector, formatted by:

@table @code
@item --io PROFILE
use a named @code{[io]} profile (format @code{dec}/@code{hex}/@code{bin}, and a
separator).  The default profile is @code{dec}.
@item --json
emit @code{@{"count": N, "result": [@dots{}]@}}, so @code{... | jq '.result'}
composes.
@item --dtype u16|s16
interpret results as unsigned or signed 16-bit (default signed).
@item -o FILE
@itemx --out FILE
write to @file{FILE} instead of standard output.
@end table
